mitigation.rag:
1. Enable and install kernel-debuginfo packages as per the instructions at https://access.redhat.com/solutions/666123.

2. Save the following SystemTap script in a file with the ".stp" extension:
   ```c
   // CVE-2018-14634
   // Theory of operations: adjust the thread's rlimit-in-effect around
   // calls to the vulnerable get_arg_page() function so as to encompass
   // the newly required _STK_LIM / 4 * 3 maximum.
   function clamp_stack_rlim_cur:long () %{
       struct rlimit *rlim = current->signal->rlim;
       unsigned long rlim_cur = READ_ONCE(rlim[RLIMIT_STACK].rlim_cur);
       unsigned long limit = _STK_LIM / 4 * 3;
       limit *= 4; // multiply it back up, to the scale used by rlim_cur
       if (rlim_cur > limit) {
           WRITE_ONCE(rlim[RLIMIT_STACK].rlim_cur, limit);
           STAP_RETURN(limit);
       } else
           STAP_RETURN(0);
   %}
   probe kernel.function("copy_strings").call {
       l = clamp_stack_rlim_cur()
       if (l) printf("lowered process %s(%d) STACK rlim_cur to %p\n", execname(), pid(), l)
   }
   probe begin { printf("CVE-2018-14634 mitigation loaded\n") }
   probe end { printf("CVE-2018-14634 mitigation unloaded\n") }
   ```

3. Install the "systemtap" package and any required dependencies. Refer to the "2. Using SystemTap" chapter in the Red Hat Enterprise Linux "SystemTap Beginners Guide" document for information on installing the required -debuginfo and matching kernel-devel packages.

4. Run the "stap -g [filename-from-step-1].stp" command as root. Note that if the host is rebooted, the changes will be lost and the script must be run again.

5. Alternatively, build the SystemTap script on a development system with "stap -g -p 4 [filename-from-step-1].stp", distribute the resulting kernel module to all affected systems, and run "staprun -L <module>" on those systems.

6. To apply a simple workaround, lower the hard limit in /etc/security/limits.conf with a line like this: `* hard stack 30720`. This will apply to user logins (e.g., via SSH), but not to various privsep child processes of services unless those services are manually restarted by someone logged in as root.

7. If someone doesn't mind rebooting with the SystemTap loaded early on at bootup, the race condition wouldn't matter anymore once both the hard and the soft limit are set on each exec. The hard limit would get inherited by all child processes, thereby preventing a concurrent setrlimit() attack on the already booted-up system.

Limitations and Side Effects:
- The SystemTap mitigation is racy and can be bypassed by a more elaborate exploit that would reset rlim[RLIMIT_STACK].rlim_cur back to a high enough value from another thread of the invoking process.
- The mitigation does not modify the live limit shown by the ulimit command.
- The workaround of lowering the hard limit in /etc/security/limits.conf may not apply to various non-login ways that a user can invoke a command.
- The mitigation may not be suitable if the application uses massive amounts of stack space.

